## What happened

.NET 11 removed the built-in DSA implementation on macOS. Apple's native crypto libraries (Security.framework / CryptoKit) never supported DSA, and earlier .NET versions used a managed or OpenSSL-based shim that has now been dropped. Calling `DSA.Create()` on macOS in .NET 11 throws a `PlatformNotSupportedException`.

This follows the pattern of progressively removing legacy algorithms — DSA was marked `[Obsolete]` (SYSLIB0030) in .NET 7.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Use OpenSSL explicitly (quick fix for verification-only):**

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

// On macOS, use DSAOpenSsl with a locally-installed OpenSSL
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = new DSAOpenSsl(); // requires OpenSSL via Homebrew/etc.
    dsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(publicKeyBytes, out _);
    bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
}
```

Ensure OpenSSL is installed (`brew install openssl`) and the native library is loadable.

**Option B — Use BouncyCastle (recommended, no native dependency):**

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var pubKey = PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes) as DsaPublicKeyParameters;
var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
verifier.Init(false, pubKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option C — Migrate away from DSA (best long-term):**

If you control both signing and verification, migrate to ECDSA or Ed25519. DSA is considered legacy and will only get harder to support.

## Summary

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| `DSAOpenSsl` | Minimal code change | Requires OpenSSL on macOS |
| BouncyCastle | Cross-platform, no native deps | Adds a dependency |
| Migrate to ECDSA | Future-proof | Requires re-signing existing data |
